# Query

Odroe Query owns asynchronous cache state. It is separate from the database
layer: a query may call HTTP, RPC, local storage, or a typed SQL driver.

## Platform-neutral client

```dart
Future<String> readTitle(int postId) async => 'Post $postId';

final client = QueryClient();
final post = await client.fetchQuery(
  QueryOptions<String>(
    key: QueryKey<String>('post', <Object?>[postId]),
    query: (_) => readTitle(postId),
  ),
);
```

`QueryKey` snapshots its JSON-like parts when it is created. Nested lists and
string-keyed maps are copied and frozen, so later changes to caller-owned
collections cannot change cache identity, prefix matching, hydration, or the
serialized key. Prefer small scalar IDs; use lists and maps only when the
resource identity is genuinely structured.

The generic argument is an exact query-cache data contract. One canonical query
entry in a `QueryClient` can hold only one exact `T`; widening `QueryKey<int>`
to `QueryKey<num>` does not permit a wider read or write. A conflicting options
registration, read, or update throws `StateError` and leaves the existing query
entry intact. Mutation keys are operation identities for defaults and filters;
they do not bind a mutation result type. Give a standalone query key an explicit
type:

```dart
final QueryKey<Post> postKey =
    QueryKey<Post>('posts.detail', <Object?>[postId]);
```

An inline key can infer `T` from `QueryOptions<T>`. The type does not participate
in canonical identity, equality, JSON, or prefix matching; use an explicit
`QueryKey<Object?>` for a standalone prefix.

Queries and infinite queries participate in `QueryClient` cancellation and
invalidation. Mutations keep separate cache, observer, scope, and retry
lifecycles; use mutation callbacks to invalidate related queries explicitly.
`QueryClient.clear()` cancels a mutation that is still paused before its next
attempt with `MutationCancelledException`, then releases its cache and online
listeners. A mutation function that already started remains application-owned:
clearing cache state cannot roll back its side effects or interrupt its Future.
Manual `QueryObserver.refetch()` returns the latest cache projection even when
the observer has no listeners. Interval polling reuses an active fetch instead
of cancelling and restarting work that lasts longer than the interval.

## Server handoff

When Query and Document share a server request, Document serializes completed
and pending Query data through the `Server`'s `Serializer`. The Flutter
`DocumentModule` uses its serializer to restore the same values before the app
takes over. The default configuration supports `DateTime`, `Duration`, `Uri`,
`BigInt`, and `Uint8List` in addition to JSON values.

For a custom wire type, keep one small serializer factory in shared source.
Use equivalent adapter sets on both runtime boundaries; no mutable global
serializer is required. `MoneyAdapter` below represents an application-owned
`SerializationAdapter` implementation.

Project-local named-record typedefs and directly declared enums are different:
the route compiler generates their codecs, so `Post`, `CreatePost`, and
`PostSort` do not need adapters. Keep an adapter for nominal classes, dependency
enums, and values with application-owned construction rules. See
[Typed RPC records and enums](/docs/guides/typed-rpc-records).

```dart
Serializer createSerializer() => Serializer(
  adapters: <SerializationAdapter<dynamic>>[MoneyAdapter()],
);

// lib/server.dart
Server createServer() => generated.createServer(
  modules: () => <QueryClientModule>[QueryClientModule.server()],
  serializer: createSerializer(),
);

// lib/main.dart
final serializer = createSerializer();
final modules = <Module>[
  QueryModule(),
  RpcModule.http(
    baseUri: rpcBaseUri(),
    serializer: serializer,
  ),
  DocumentModule(serializer: serializer),
];
```

The client uses the same [cross-platform RPC origin helper](/docs/guides/server-operations#client-origin)
as the application composition root.

The same serializer contract covers the initial handoff and later streamed
Query frames. A custom tag available on only one side is rejected instead of
silently changing the value type.

Serialization deliberately erases the Dart type token. When the target cache
has no entry, hydration restores a dynamic placeholder. Completed data is
validated before a typed read or adoption; a mismatch reports `StateError` and
preserves that placeholder. Pending options may adopt immediately and transfer
the Future; its eventual value is then validated, and a mismatch leaves the
typed query in error. Hydration into an existing typed query validates against
that entry directly.

## Flutter

Install `QueryModule` from `query_flutter.dart`, then use focused builders for
query, infinite-query, or mutation state. The query function can call a
generated RPC ref without coupling either subsystem. Keep the options object in
the owning `State`; ordinary widget rebuilds should reuse its identity.

### Keep page bounds across every layer

The full-stack product returns `PostPage` with at most the requested `items`
and a nullable `nextCursor`. Its SQL handler validates `limit`, applies a strict
ID keyset predicate, and reads one extra row. Generated RPC preserves that
record, and Infinite Query feeds only `nextCursor` into the next request:

```dart
final options = InfiniteQueryOptions<PostPage, int?>(
  key: QueryKey<InfiniteData<PostPage, int?>>('posts.list'),
  initialPageParam: null,
  getNextPageParam: (lastPage, _, _, _) => lastPage.nextCursor,
  query: (page) => routes.listPosts(
    rpc,
    (
      cursor: page.pageParam,
      ids: const <int>[],
      limit: 20,
      sort: PostSort.newest,
    ),
    cancelled: page.query.cancelToken.whenCancelled.then<void>((_) {}),
  ),
);
```

`InfiniteQueryBuilder<PostPage, int?>` renders the ordinary state through
`result.query`, flattens `query.requireData.pages`, and exposes Load more only
when `result.hasNextPage`. A `null` cursor ends pagination without an implicit
count or a client-side length guess. See
[Query workflows](/docs/guides/query-workflows) for the widget lifecycle.

The starter leaves `maxPages` unset so Load more never silently removes rows the
user already loaded. Each database read and RPC frame is still bounded, but the
client cache grows by one page per explicit action. Long-lived feeds should set
`maxPages` only together with previous-page navigation or a deliberate windowed
UI; independently virtualize rendering when retaining many loaded rows.

### Observe one query

The same ownership rule applies to an ordinary detail query:

```dart
late QueryOptions<Post> _postOptions;

@override
void initState() {
  super.initState();
  _postOptions = _createPostOptions();
}

@override
void didUpdateWidget(PostDetailPage oldWidget) {
  super.didUpdateWidget(oldWidget);
  if (oldWidget.postId != widget.postId ||
      !identical(oldWidget.rpc, widget.rpc)) {
    _postOptions = _createPostOptions();
  }
}

QueryOptions<Post> _createPostOptions() {
  final postId = widget.postId;
  final rpc = widget.rpc;
  return QueryOptions<Post>(
    key: QueryKey<Post>('posts.detail', <Object?>[postId]),
    query: (context) => generated.routes.posts.postId.readPost(
      rpc,
      postId,
      cancelled: context.cancelToken.whenCancelled.then<void>((_) {}),
    ),
  );
}

@override
Widget build(BuildContext context) => QueryBuilder<Post>(
  options: _postOptions,
  builder: (_, result) {
    if (result.isError && !result.hasData) {
      return const Text('Unable to load post');
    }
    if (!result.hasData) return const CircularProgressIndicator();
    return Text(result.requireData.title);
  },
);
```

Options identity is part of the observer lifecycle, not temporary build output.
Replace it when a real query input or captured client changes, as above; keep
the same instance for theme, layout, parent, and unrelated state rebuilds.

Reading `context.cancelToken` marks the operation as cancellation-aware. An
explicit `cancelQueries` call, or removal of the last observer while the fetch
is active, then reaches `HttpTransport` and aborts the request. RPC exposes the
result as `RpcCancelledException`, while Query keeps cancellation out of error
state.

Persistence is an explicit adapter. Installing Query does not silently choose
disk storage or a network transport.
